{
  "name": "Study shows babies born to healthy mothers worldwide are similar in size",
  "parent_id": 203313642253074,
  "group_id": "df6142cf-4a60-4e63-b2ec-7bdb40e97df5",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-30T14:54:08.193Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-07-30T14:54:08.275Z",
  "published_at": "2026-07-30T14:54:08.261Z",
  "id": 203709023041329,
  "uuid": "3eb72fc4-dfde-4faa-ac5d-9f56d021b8e1",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "037c1de5-0146-4437-aa34-ca83569a3308",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "565e2f02-5304-a88e-470e-629c65d7c560",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "This landmark international study, INTERGROWTH-21st, was led by Oxford University researchers, which involved almost 60,000 pregnancies in eight defined urban areas in Kenya, Brazil, China, India, Italy, Oman, the UK and USA.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Maternal health, education and nutrition are the primary determinants of size of a baby at birth, not race and ethnicity, concluded the researchers, including Dr Maria Carvalho from Aga Khan University's Faculty of Health Sciences in Nairobi, and Professor Zulfiqar Bhutta from AKU's Centre for Excellence in Women and Child Health in Karachi and the Hospital for Sick Children in Toronto. Dr Bhutta is also the Chair of the Steering Committee of the research team.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Funded by the Melinda and Bill Gates Foundation and published in The Lancet, the study also demonstrates that it is important to improve women's education, health and nutritional status as this has a significant bearing on the healthy growth of the baby in the womb and on future good health.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The INTERGROWTH-21st results fit perfectly with the existing WHO standards being used for infants. In short, from now on international standards can be used to make judgments on growth and size from conception to 5 years worldwide, as it is the case for most conditions in medicine.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Several Kenyan mums contributed to the study run by the Aga Khan University at the University Hospital, with additional data from the M P Shah Hospital and the Avenue Hospital in Nairobi. Aga Khan University Hospital is a leading tertiary referral hospital in East Africa and has a strong research department that supports and helps specialists-in-training with all their research projects.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
